ncy programs), and where we win and lose - grounded in win/loss analysis and field feedback.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Drive product launches and releases</strong>. Turn platform capabilities and new workflow coverage into launch narratives, internal enablement, and external announcements that land with executive buyers.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Fuel demand and thought leadership</strong>. Supply campaigns, events, and content marketing with differentiated angles, from executive-facing reports to industry-specific value narratives.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Be the voice of the market internally.</strong> Bring buyer, analyst, and competitive insight back into product and leadership discussions, even when it complicates the roadmap story, to sharpen framing and packaging.</p></li></ul><div style="min-height:1.2em;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px"> </div><h2>What you'll bring</h2><ul style="min-height:1.5em"><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">5–8 years in B2B software marketing, with at least 3 years in product marketing for enterprise SaaS - ideally in ERP-adjacent, finance operations, automation, or applied-AI categories.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Proven ability to market to economic buyers: you write for CFOs, CIOs/CTOs, and COOs in the language of cost, productivity, working capital, and EBIT - not features.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Strong competitive instincts: experience building battlecards and positioning in crowded categories, and translating technical differentiation into commercial arguments.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">A portfolio of enablement assets that sales teams actually used - you measure your work by win rates and message adoption, not asset counts.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Analytical rigor: comfortable with win/loss analysis, market and analyst research, and grounding messaging claims in defensible evidence.</p></li><li><p style=